# Everything below except the two API variables is read on the server at request time,
# so a prebuilt image is configured entirely by the environment it starts in - no
# rebuild, and no value baked into anything the browser downloads. The older
# `NEXT_PUBLIC_`-prefixed spelling of each name still works as a fallback, for builds
# that already set it; the unprefixed name wins where both are present.

# -------- Where the API is --------
#
# Two variables, and which one you need depends on whether the browser talks to the API
# directly or through this app.
#
# A deployed instance uses neither. Unset, the browser calls `/api/v1` on the same origin
# that served the page, and the catch-all route handler at `app/api/v1/[...path]/route.ts`
# forwards each request to the API container. That is what lets one prebuilt image run on
# any domain with no rebuild - and it means no CORS to configure and no cross-site cookie
# to lose.

# The API container's address, read by that route handler on every request. An *origin*,
# with no `/api/v1` on the end: the request's own path is appended to it unchanged, so a
# prefix here would be doubled. The default is the compose service name, which is why the
# shipped bundle sets nothing; `next dev` falls back to `http://localhost:8000` instead.
# API_INTERNAL_URL=http://api:8000

# The browser's own API base, and the one variable here that is genuinely baked into the
# bundle at build time - `NEXT_PUBLIC_*` is inlined by the compiler where it is written as
# a literal, so changing it means rebuilding. Set it only for a split-origin deployment,
# where the API answers on a host of its own and the proxy route above is bypassed
# entirely. `src/proxy.ts` reduces it to an origin for the CSP's `connect-src`, so the
# path is harmless there.
#
# Local development is the everyday case for setting it: this points the browser straight
# at the API's own published port, which is how the stack has always run, and
# `scripts/screenshots.mjs` reads the same variable from Node, where an absolute URL is
# the only kind that works.
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=http://localhost:8000/api/v1

# Example split-origin production value (uncomment and modify)
# NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=https://api.opendiving.app/api/v1

# -------- This instance --------

# The origin this instance is reached at, used for the `og:`/`twitter:` tags and for
# resolving relative URLs in page metadata. Nothing functional depends on it - get it
# wrong and link previews point at the wrong host, that is all - but the default is
# `http://localhost:3000`, which is nobody's public address, so set it. An absolute
# origin: scheme and host, no trailing path.
# SITE_URL=https://dives.example.com

# Optional. Address offered on /contact as a fallback if a form submission fails.
# Display only - the form posts to the API, which decides where the message actually
# goes (CONTACT_FORM_EMAIL there); setting this does not change delivery. Leave it
# unset unless it matches that address: a failed submission then points at the GitHub
# issue tracker, which is never the wrong advice.
# CONTACT_EMAIL=contact@opendiving.app

# Whether avatars are loaded from Gravatar. Off unless you say otherwise, and worth
# knowing before you say otherwise: it is the only third-party request the app makes
# from the browser, and it discloses a hash of every signed-in user's email address,
# along with their IP address, to Automattic - on every page with the account menu on
# it, which is every page. Left off, the app draws each user's initials and contacts
# nobody. Turning it on adds a section to the privacy page saying so.
# GRAVATAR_ENABLED=true

# Google Sign-In (OAuth 2.0 client ID from the Google Cloud Console, "Web application"
# type). Not a secret - safe to expose to the browser. Leave unset to hide the
# "Continue with Google" button, which also drops `accounts.google.com` from the CSP.
# Must match the API's GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID.
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=your-client-id.apps.googleusercontent.com

# -------- Map tiles (the dive site dialog's "Pick on a map") --------
# Raster tile templates, in `{z}/{x}/{y}` form. Both default to Carto's Positron and
# Dark Matter, which are OpenStreetMap data restyled: keyless, free, and with a dark
# variant that matches the app's theme, which OSM's own tiles don't have.
#
# The host is the one and only thing the map adds to the CSP - it's derived from these
# variables into `img-src` by `src/proxy.ts`, so a tile host hardcoded anywhere else is
# blocked rather than merely misconfigured. Give a plain host with no `{s}` subdomain
# placeholder: sharding is an HTTP/1.1 workaround, and one fixed host is one exact CSP
# source instead of a wildcard.
# MAP_TILE_URL=https://basemaps.cartocdn.com/light_all/{z}/{x}/{y}.png
# Falls back to MAP_TILE_URL when unset - so pointing the light one at your own tile
# server and leaving this alone means "use mine", not "use mine by day and a
# stranger's by night".
# MAP_TILE_URL_DARK=https://basemaps.cartocdn.com/dark_all/{z}/{x}/{y}.png
# Rendered over the map. For OpenStreetMap-derived tiles, crediting is a licence
# condition - change this whenever you change the tile URLs above. `[label](url)`
# becomes a link, so the credit can point at the licence rather than just naming it;
# anything outside that syntax renders as plain text, and a link that isn't http(s)
# keeps its label and loses its href.
# MAP_TILE_ATTRIBUTION="[© OpenStreetMap contributors](https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright) [© CARTO](https://carto.com/attributions)"
#
# Worth knowing before you leave the default in place: tiles are fetched by the browser,
# so opening the picker tells the tile host your users' IP addresses and the z/x/y of the
# area they're looking at - which, for a dive log, is roughly where their sites are. It's
# the tile coordinates only; `Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin` (set in
# `next.config.js`) keeps the site UUID out of the `Referer` header. Nothing else about
# the app leaves the machine. If that trade doesn't suit your instance, point these at
# your own tile server - it's the one place self-hosting buys real privacy here, and the
# CSP follows the variable automatically.
